course: dictionary
lesson: 1060
title: Bother: one word, many jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word bother can do different jobs. It can be a verb, a noun, or a word said on its own. The words around it show you which meaning you need, so read the clues first.
explain: «Don't bother me while I'm reading.» Here bother means annoy or interrupt. «What's bothering you?» is different: it means what is worrying you. «It's no bother» means it is no trouble at all.
explain: To check your answer, swap your meaning into the sentence. Try "annoy", "worry" or "trouble" and see if the sentence still makes sense. If it does, you have probably found the right meaning.
fact: Bother can be a verb, a noun, or a word said on its own.
fact: As a verb, «bother» can mean to annoy, to interrupt, or to worry.
fact: «Can't be bothered» means you do not want to make the effort.
fact: «Bother!» is a mild, old-fashioned word said when something goes wrong.
fact: If anything online or anyone bothers you, tell a trusted adult.
exercise: «Don't bother me while I'm reading.» What does bother mean here? | To make a loud noise | To run very fast | To give a present | *To annoy or interrupt someone
exercise: «It's no bother.» What does bother mean in this sentence? | A happy song | A large animal | A kind of food | *Trouble or effort
exercise: «He didn't bother to wash his hands.» What does bother mean here? | To worry about something | *To make the effort to do something | To shout very loudly | To count coins
test: «Sorry to bother you, but could you help me find my bag?» Why does the speaker say sorry? | Because they are very rich | Because they like to shout | Because they lost the bag on purpose | *Because asking might interrupt or trouble someone
test: Jo says «Bother!» when she drops her ice cream. What does this show? | She is singing a song | She is delighted with her ice cream | She is asking for more ice cream | *She is a bit cross that something went wrong
exam: «The animals above don't bother themselves about the past.» What does bother mean here? | *To worry about or think about | To sing loudly | To buy a new coat | To dig a hole
